{
  "duration": 459.98400000001294,
  "chapters": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 87.96000000000244
    },
    {
      "start": 87.96000000000244,
      "end": 177.40800000000493
    },
    {
      "start": 177.40800000000493,
      "end": 265.2720000000074
    },
    {
      "start": 265.2720000000074,
      "end": 352.9200000000098
    },
    {
      "start": 352.9200000000098,
      "end": 459.98400000001294
    }
  ],
  "cues": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 1.2999999523162842,
      "text": "Hey, welcome back.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 1.940000057220459,
      "end": 5.579999923706055,
      "text": "Last time, a changing magnetic field created an induced emf.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 6.179999828338623,
      "end": 9.300000190734863,
      "text": "Today the field can stay constant while a conductor moves.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 10.319999694824219,
      "end": 14.65999984741211,
      "text": "Picture a vertical metal rod moving to the right through a uniform field into",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 14.65999984741211,
      "end": 15.140000343322754,
      "text": "the page.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 16.139999389648438,
      "end": 17.84000015258789,
      "text": "Charges inside the rod share its motion.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 18.579999923706055,
      "end": 22.739999771118164,
      "text": "The magnetic force on a positive charge is q times velocity cross B.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 23.540000915527344,
      "end": 27.020000457763672,
      "text": "Use your right hand: right crossed with into the page points upward.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 28.059999465942383,
      "end": 31.920000076293945,
      "text": "Positive charge accumulates near the upper end, and electrons shift toward the",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 31.920000076293945,
      "end": 32.36000061035156,
      "text": "lower end.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 33.02000045776367,
      "end": 34.560001373291016,
      "text": "The upper end becomes positive.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 35.34000015258789,
      "end": 37.599998474121094,
      "text": "This separation creates a downward electric field.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 38.29999923706055,
      "end": 42.459999084472656,
      "text": "In the ideal open circuit steady state, electric and magnetic forces balance",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 42.459999084472656,
      "end": 43.15999984741211,
      "text": "along the rod.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 43.84000015258789,
      "end": 48.63999938964844,
      "text": "The electric field magnitude is v B, so the voltage magnitude across a rod of",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 48.63999938964844,
      "end": 51,
      "text": "length l is B l v.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 51,
      "end": 54.97999954223633,
      "text": "This shortcut assumes the rod, velocity and field are mutually perpendicular",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 55.439998626708984,
      "end": 57.400001525878906,
      "text": "and the motion is uniform across the rod.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 57.959999084472656,
      "end": 61.959999084472656,
      "text": "More generally, the motional contribution is the line integral of velocity",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 61.959999084472656,
      "end": 64.13999938964844,
      "text": "cross B dotted with the length element.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 64.94000244140625,
      "end": 68.12000274658203,
      "text": "Do not multiply three magnitudes when the geometry does not support it.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 68.86000061035156,
      "end": 73.36000061035156,
      "text": "For B equal to zero point five tesla, length zero point four meter and speed",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 73.36000061035156,
      "end": 76.68000030517578,
      "text": "three meters per second, the voltage is zero point six volt.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 77.37999725341797,
      "end": 81.30000305175781,
      "text": "There is no complete external circuit, so no sustained circulating current.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 82.18000030517578,
      "end": 83.80000305175781,
      "text": "Voltage and current are different questions.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 84.69999694824219,
      "end": 87.77999877929688,
      "text": "Reverse the motion or the field, and the end polarity reverses.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 87.96000000000244,
      "end": 92.4715383771774,
      "text": "Now place the same moving rod across two horizontal conducting rails,",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 92.4715383771774,
      "end": 93.059999904635,
      "text": "connected",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 93.059999904635,
      "end": 94.96000000000244,
      "text": "through a resistor on the left.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 95.61999984741455,
      "end": 96.80000015259033,
      "text": "The rod completes a circuit.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 97.60000034332519,
      "end": 100.38000007629638,
      "text": "As it moves right, the enclosed rectangle grows wider.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 101.11999984741455,
      "end": 106.35999961853271,
      "text": "Its area is rod length times position x, so the magnitude of flux is B times l",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 106.35999961853271,
      "end": 107.06000038147216,
      "text": "times x.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 108.06000038147216,
      "end": 109.30000015259033,
      "text": "Differentiate with respect to time.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 109.90000053406006,
      "end": 115.67999931335693,
      "text": "Since the rate of change of x is speed v, the induced emf magnitude is B l v.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 115.67999931335693,
      "end": 118.56000038147216,
      "text": "The charge force picture and the changing flux picture agree.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 119.25999923706298,
      "end": 120.43999954223877,
      "text": "Let's get direction without guessing.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 121.11999984741455,
      "end": 123.820000610354,
      "text": "The field goes into the page, and the circuit area grows.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 124.5799989318872,
      "end": 125.55999847412353,
      "text": "Inward flux is increasing.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 126.37999816894775,
      "end": 129.66000076294188,
      "text": "Lenz's law requires an outward induced field, which corresponds to",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 129.66000076294188,
      "end": 132.68000122070555,
      "text": "counterclockwise conventional current when viewed facing the page.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 133.68000122070555,
      "end": 136.16000076294188,
      "text": "Along the right hand moving rod, that current goes upward.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 136.77999969482664,
      "end": 138.36000152588133,
      "text": "Along the top rail it goes left.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 138.75999923706297,
      "end": 143.1400003051782,
      "text": "Use our previous numbers: field zero point five tesla, rod length zero point",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 143.1400003051782,
      "end": 145.70000167846922,
      "text": "four meter and speed three meters per second.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 146.66000076294188,
      "end": 148.2399987792993,
      "text": "Emf is zero point six volt.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 149.25999923706297,
      "end": 153.18000122070555,
      "text": "If the total circuit resistance is two ohms, current is zero point three",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 153.18000122070555,
      "end": 153.69999786377196,
      "text": "ampere.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 154.51999755859617,
      "end": 158.40000244140867,
      "text": "We assume good sliding contacts, negligible lead resistance beyond the stated",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 158.40000244140867,
      "end": 160.87999816894774,
      "text": "total, and negligible self inductive effects.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 161.6200036621118,
      "end": 165.2399987792993,
      "text": "Immediately after a sudden change, appreciable inductance could alter the",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 165.2399987792993,
      "end": 165.8399972534204,
      "text": "current response.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 166.320000610354,
      "end": 170.40000244140867,
      "text": "In this elementary resistive model, emf divided by resistance is appropriate.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 171.68000122070555,
      "end": 174.820000610354,
      "text": "Opening the connection stops the sustained loop current even though the moving",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 174.820000610354,
      "end": 176.90000244140867,
      "text": "rod can still develop an end to end voltage.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 177.40800000000493,
      "end": 181.3480000572254,
      "text": "Current is now flowing, so the rod experiences a magnetic force.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 182.26800013351934,
      "end": 184.46799994278447,
      "text": "Current points up and field points into the page.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 185.1479997711231,
      "end": 187.06799984741704,
      "text": "Up crossed with into the page points left.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 187.72799969482915,
      "end": 190.0480003433277,
      "text": "That force opposes the rod's rightward motion.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 190.76799965668218,
      "end": 194.72799969482915,
      "text": "To maintain constant speed, an external agent must pull right with equal",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 194.72799969482915,
      "end": 197.5880003051807,
      "text": "magnitude, if friction and other forces are neglected.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 198.54799938965337,
      "end": 202.16800022888677,
      "text": "The magnetic force magnitude is current times rod length times field.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 202.78799916077153,
      "end": 208.0880003051807,
      "text": "Substitute current B l v over R and you obtain B squared times l squared times",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 208.0880003051807,
      "end": 208.96799946594732,
      "text": "v over R.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 209.94800091553228,
      "end": 211.4280004577686,
      "text": "Let's calculate with the same circuit.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 212.34799862671392,
      "end": 216.48440946217565,
      "text": "Current is zero point three ampere, length zero point four meter and field",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 216.48440946217565,
      "end": 216.70799923706548,
      "text": "zero",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 216.70799923706548,
      "end": 217.6879987793018,
      "text": "point five tesla.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 218.50799847412603,
      "end": 220.44800091553228,
      "text": "The drag force is zero point zero six newton.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 221.20799923706548,
      "end": 224.72799969482915,
      "text": "At speed three meters per second, the pulling power is zero point one eight",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 224.72799969482915,
      "end": 225.0279989318897,
      "text": "watt.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 225.80800152588384,
      "end": 229.24800015259282,
      "text": "Now calculate electrical heating: current squared times resistance.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 230.04799938965337,
      "end": 233.34799862671392,
      "text": "Zero point three squared times two is also zero point one eight watt.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 233.34799862671392,
      "end": 236.0279989318897,
      "text": "Lovely: the energy accounts match.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 236.72799969482915,
      "end": 240.1879987793018,
      "text": "The resistor's heating comes from mechanical work, not an inexhaustible",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 240.1879987793018,
      "end": 241.24800015259282,
      "text": "magnetic energy gift.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 241.90800000000493,
      "end": 244.50799847412603,
      "text": "At constant speed, kinetic energy is unchanged.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 245.12800122070806,
      "end": 249.46799755859868,
      "text": "If you release the rod, this simple model predicts deceleration as mechanical",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 249.46799755859868,
      "end": 250.92799664307134,
      "text": "energy is converted into heat.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 251.20800305176274,
      "end": 254.88800335693853,
      "text": "The drag decreases with speed; it is not a fixed friction force.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 254.88800335693853,
      "end": 259.62800122070803,
      "text": "Doubling speed with the other quantities fixed doubles current and force, but",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 259.62800122070803,
      "end": 260.7879972534229,
      "text": "quadruples heating power.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 261.46799755859865,
      "end": 264.1680021362354,
      "text": "Those different scaling laws are useful JEE checks.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 265.2720000000074,
      "end": 267.2919999809339,
      "text": "Here is a trap worth slowing down for.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 267.93200008583807,
      "end": 271.4719998092725,
      "text": "A rectangular conducting loop moves right into a region with uniform magnetic",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 271.4719998092725,
      "end": 272.6120001525953,
      "text": "field into the page.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 273.45200030518316,
      "end": 275.8320004196241,
      "text": "Does motion always mean a nonzero net emf?",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 276.39199988556646,
      "end": 276.73200003815435,
      "text": "No.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 277.4719998092725,
      "end": 280.1520001144483,
      "text": "During entry, the area of the loop inside the field increases.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 280.79200045777105,
      "end": 285.31302550409515,
      "text": "If its vertical side length is w, the overlapping area grows at rate w times",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 285.31302550409515,
      "end": 285.4319998474195,
      "text": "v.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 286.0719992370679,
      "end": 289.2720000000074,
      "text": "The emf magnitude is B w v.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 289.9919993133619,
      "end": 294.0573159119885,
      "text": "Inward flux increases, so current is counterclockwise as viewed facing the",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 294.0573159119885,
      "end": 294.33199946594976,
      "text": "page.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 294.79200045777105,
      "end": 300.0120016784742,
      "text": "Once the entire rigid loop is inside the uniform, static field, its area and",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 300.0120016784742,
      "end": 300.79200045777105,
      "text": "orientation remain fixed.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 301.55199877930426,
      "end": 304.4119993896558,
      "text": "Flux is constant, so net induced emf is zero.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 305.29200045777105,
      "end": 307.95200030518316,
      "text": "Opposite side motional contributions cancel around the loop.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 308.8120009155347,
      "end": 312.29200045777105,
      "text": "Charges may redistribute locally, but this does not create a sustained loop",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 312.29200045777105,
      "end": 314.2519995422437,
      "text": "current in our resistive steady state model.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 314.89199893189215,
      "end": 317.23199908448004,
      "text": "As the loop exits, inward flux decreases.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 317.85200183106207,
      "end": 319.05199877930426,
      "text": "The current becomes clockwise.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 319.6919981689527,
      "end": 324.1919981689527,
      "text": "Try field zero point four tesla, vertical width zero point three meter and",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 324.1919981689527,
      "end": 325.3719984741285,
      "text": "speed two meters per second.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 326.35200183106207,
      "end": 329.5119978637769,
      "text": "Entry or exit emf magnitude is zero point two four volt.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 330.53200213623785,
      "end": 334.6919981689527,
      "text": "With total resistance one point two ohms and negligible inductance, current",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 334.6919981689527,
      "end": 336.752003356941,
      "text": "magnitude is zero point two ampere.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 337.15199725342535,
      "end": 338.55199877930426,
      "text": "Fully inside, current is zero.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 339.1720015258863,
      "end": 343.03200213623785,
      "text": "The ideal abrupt boundaries separate three regimes; real field edges are",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 343.03200213623785,
      "end": 343.2919966430738,
      "text": "smoother.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 344.0720030517652,
      "end": 347.74071928680377,
      "text": "Mechanical drag acts while flux is changing, not throughout every uniform",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 347.74071928680377,
      "end": 347.9920012207105,
      "text": "field",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 347.9920012207105,
      "end": 348.53200213623785,
      "text": "translation.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 349.35200183106207,
      "end": 352.8120009155347,
      "text": "Check overlap area before using B l v for the net circuit emf.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 352.9200000000098,
      "end": 355.66000000954654,
      "text": "A circuit does not always need visible wires.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 356.16000000954654,
      "end": 359.9000000190833,
      "text": "A solid metal plate contains many possible closed conducting paths.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 360.7800001335242,
      "end": 365.3599995803931,
      "text": "If linked magnetic flux changes through those paths, circulating currents can",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 365.3599995803931,
      "end": 366.5799998474219,
      "text": "form inside the plate.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 367.27999965668704,
      "end": 368.68000022889163,
      "text": "We call them eddy currents.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 369.68000022889163,
      "end": 372.94000045777346,
      "text": "Picture a conducting plate moving through a localized magnetic field.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 373.99999992371585,
      "end": 378.3999995422461,
      "text": "Different parts enter and leave the field region, so the linked flux changes.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 378.87999908448245,
      "end": 382.87999908448245,
      "text": "The induced currents interact with the field and can oppose the motion,",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 383.78000061036136,
      "end": 385.53999893189456,
      "text": "converting mechanical energy into heat.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 386.37999908448245,
      "end": 389.4200000000098,
      "text": "This gives magnetic damping without rubbing surfaces together.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 390.1600016784766,
      "end": 394.8599986267188,
      "text": "But do not claim that any metal moving anywhere in a uniform static field must",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 394.8599986267188,
      "end": 397.37999908448245,
      "text": "experience eddy current braking.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 397.37999908448245,
      "end": 399.5000018310645,
      "text": "Geometry and changing linked flux matter.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 400.179998321543,
      "end": 404.239999694834,
      "text": "A stationary ordinary conductor in a static field does not continuously",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 404.239999694834,
      "end": 405.3200015258887,
      "text": "generate eddy currents.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 405.3200015258887,
      "end": 409.179998321543,
      "text": "A changing applied field can induce them even without motion.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 410.19999877930667,
      "end": 414.4800013733008,
      "text": "For transformer cores, unwanted circulating currents waste energy as heat.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 415.5000018310645,
      "end": 419.87999908448245,
      "text": "Thin insulated laminations interrupt large conducting paths and reduce those",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 419.87999908448245,
      "end": 420.05999938965823,
      "text": "losses.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 421.19999877930667,
      "end": 424.95025768965695,
      "text": "Slots in a conducting plate can similarly weaken damping by obstructing",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 424.95025768965695,
      "end": 425.3200015258887,
      "text": "current",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 425.3200015258887,
      "end": 425.68000213624026,
      "text": "paths.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 426.4399966430762,
      "end": 430.19999877930667,
      "text": "These methods reduce currents; they do not abolish electromagnetic induction",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 430.19999877930667,
      "end": 430.78000061036136,
      "text": "itself.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 431.5000018310645,
      "end": 436.4399966430762,
      "text": "Induction heating deliberately uses changing fields to transfer energy into a",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 436.4399966430762,
      "end": 437.0000018310645,
      "text": "conducting workpiece.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 437.68000213624026,
      "end": 439.4000033569434,
      "text": "The power supply provides that energy.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 440.119996948252,
      "end": 444.9200000000098,
      "text": "We will not assign one universal B l v circuit to an entire plate: its current",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 444.9200000000098,
      "end": 448.7200030517676,
      "text": "distribution depends on shape, conductivity and field variation.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 449.5199984741309,
      "end": 454.55999938965823,
      "text": "Your final checklist is motion geometry, flux change, closed paths, resistance",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 454.7200030517676,
      "end": 455.8200015258887,
      "text": "and the energy source.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 455.8200015258887,
      "end": 459.739999694834,
      "text": "That is how the rod and the plate belong to the same physical story.",
      "chapter": 4
    }
  ]
}
